---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/intermediate/sql-for-analysts">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    SQL is <strong>the analyst's primary interface to data</strong>. It is
    <em>declarative</em> — you describe the result you want and the engine finds a plan
    to produce it, a design Edgar Codd's relational model (1970) made possible and
    fifty years of optimisation made fast — and it is everywhere: warehouses, lakes,
    database-backed spreadsheets, even the query layers under modern big-data tools.
    Analyst-level SQL goes beyond SELECT: <strong>joins define your population</strong>,
    <strong>GROUP BY defines your granularity</strong>, and <strong>window functions
    unlock the questions</strong> that older SQL made painful. The mental shift from
    spreadsheet thinking is the lesson's real content: in SQL you work in sets of rows,
    and every operation transforms one set into another.
  </p>
  <p>
    That mental shift has a flip side that makes SQL dangerous as well as powerful:
    wrong SQL rarely errors. A bad join returns a big, plausible-looking table; a
    mis-specified filter quietly narrows the population. So this lesson pairs each
    technique with the habit that keeps it honest — starting with the habit of habits:
    at every step, know <em>what one row represents</em>.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four techniques that cover most analyst work" widget="equationStep" config={sqlCfg} />

  <h2>SELECT, WHERE, and the relational frame</h2>
  <p>
    The frame first. A relational database stores data in <strong>tables</strong> —
    rows are records, columns are fields, and keys connect tables. A query moves
    through a fixed logical order you can internalise as a pipeline:
    <strong>FROM</strong> (which tables, joined how) → <strong>WHERE</strong> (which
    rows survive) → <strong>GROUP BY</strong> (which rows collapse together) →
    <strong>HAVING</strong> (which groups survive) → <strong>SELECT</strong> (which
    columns and computed expressions appear) → <strong>ORDER BY / LIMIT</strong>
    (presentation). Knowing that evaluation order explains the classic beginner
    errors — you cannot filter on a column alias in WHERE (aliases are born at
    SELECT, after WHERE runs), and WHERE cannot see aggregates (that is HAVING's
    job). Worked basics: <code>SELECT region, amount FROM orders WHERE
    status = 'completed' AND amount is greater than 0 ORDER BY amount DESC LIMIT 20</code>
    — filter to completed, positive-amount orders, show the twenty largest.
    Every analysis, however long, is this pipeline repeated and layered.
  </p>

  <h2>Grain: the question behind every query</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Grain</strong> is what one row represents, and it changes as you query:
    raw orders are one row per order; after a join to items, one row per order-item
    line; after GROUP BY month, one row per month. Silently changing grain is how
    SQL produces wrong numbers that look right. The canonical case: joining
    <code>orders</code> (one row per order) to <code>order_items</code> (three rows
    per order) and then summing a shipping fee that lives on the order — the fee
    counts three times, revenue inflates by the average items-per-order, and no
    error message appears. The defences are mechanical: state the grain in a
    comment at the top of each CTE; check row counts at each step
    (<code>SELECT COUNT(*)</code> is the analyst's pulse); aggregate the many-side
    <em>before</em> joining when the measure belongs to the one-side (sum items per
    order first, then join to orders — the fan-out never happens). Analysts who
    internalise grain stop making the most common silent mistake in the craft.
  </p>

  <h2>Joins: choosing your population</h2>
  <p>
    A join stitches tables on a key — and the join <em>type</em> is a population
    decision. <strong>INNER JOIN</strong> keeps only rows with matches on both
    sides: join orders to customers and you analyse only orders that have a
    customer record — silently excluding orphans. <strong>LEFT JOIN</strong> keeps
    every left row, filling the right with NULLs where there was no match: join
    customers to orders with a left join and you keep the customers who never
    ordered — which is exactly the population churn analysis needs. (RIGHT JOIN
    is LEFT with the tables swapped; FULL OUTER keeps everything from both.)
    Worked case: "count customers with no orders in 2024" — left join
    customers to 2024's orders, keep rows where the order ID is NULL, count
    distinct customers. The pattern "<code>WHERE right.key IS NULL</code>" after a
    left join is the standard anti-join, and it only works if you remember that
    the NULL filter <em>after</em> a left join is not the same as an inner join
    with the filter — the left join made the missingness visible; the inner join
    deleted it. Finally, check every join's key for uniqueness on the side that
    should be unique — a "unique" customers table with two rows for customer 7
    is the fan-out factory from the grain section.
  </p>

  <h2>GROUP BY: the aggregation lens</h2>
  <p>
    GROUP BY collapses rows into groups and computes one value per group — the
    workhorse of every business question: revenue per month, users per country,
    defects per production line. The mechanics: every column in SELECT must be
    either in the GROUP BY or inside an aggregate (COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX) —
    the database will refuse anything else, which is a feature: it forces you to
    declare what the one-row-per-group output means. <strong>WHERE filters rows
    before grouping; HAVING filters groups after</strong> — "completed orders
    over 100" is WHERE, "regions whose total exceeds 10,000" is HAVING. Worked
    query with the grain habit visible: <code>WITH monthly AS (SELECT
    date_trunc('month', order_date) AS month, SUM(amount) AS revenue FROM orders
    WHERE status = 'completed' GROUP BY 1) SELECT * FROM monthly ORDER BY
    month</code> — filter early, group once, sort late. One refinement that
    separates careful analysts: COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(column) counts
    non-null values — the difference between "orders received" and "orders with a
    known channel", and a denominator decision that changes every rate you
    compute.
  </p>

  <h2>NULL semantics: not zero, not false, not equal to anything</h2>
  <p>
    NULL means "no value", and it behaves unlike any other value: <code>NULL =
    NULL</code> is not true (it is NULL — unknown), so equality filters silently
    exclude nulls; <code>amount is greater than 0</code> excludes NULL amounts
    without your asking; NOT IN with a NULL in the list returns nothing at all
    (the famous trap). Aggregates skip NULLs: AVG ignores them in both sum and
    count, so "average sale" quietly becomes "average of known sales" — the
    cleaning lesson's missingness problem, resurfacing in a WHERE clause. The
    toolkit: <code>IS NULL / IS NOT NULL</code> for testing (never <code>= NULL</code>);
    <code>COALESCE(a, b)</code> to substitute a default when the first value is
    missing — used thoughtfully, because "no sale" and "sale of 0" are different
    facts that a blanket COALESCE erases. The habit: for any metric, ask whether
    NULLs changed the numerator, the denominator, or the population — all three
    are possible, all three are silent.
  </p>

  <h2>Window functions: aggregates without collapsing</h2>
  <p>
    Window functions compute across rows <em>while keeping the rows</em> — the
    feature that made modern analytical SQL possible. The syntax declares the
    window: <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY region ORDER BY sales DESC)</code>
    — number the rows within each region, best first; <code>SUM(amount) OVER
    (ORDER BY day)</code> — a running total; <code>AVG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY
    region)</code> — each row carries its group's average alongside its own value.
    The two canonical analysts' questions are now one-liners. <strong>Top 3 per
    group</strong>: rank within region, then keep ranks 1–3 in an outer filter —
    impossible with GROUP BY (which collapses the products away). <strong>Period
    over period</strong>: <code>revenue − LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month)</code>
    puts last month's number on this month's row, and the growth rate is one
    division away. Add PERCENT_RANK, NTILE (deciles), and moving-average frames
    (<code>ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code>) and most dashboard
    mathematics lives at this level. The grain habit still applies — a window's
    PARTITION BY is a shadow GROUP BY whose grain you must be able to state.
  </p>

  <h2>CTEs and readable SQL</h2>
  <p>
    Long analyses are pipelines, and <strong>common table expressions</strong>
    (CTEs) are how you write them like code: <code>WITH completed AS (…), monthly
    AS (SELECT … FROM completed …), ranked AS (…)</code> — each step named,
    commented, and one purpose wide. The benefits compound: each CTE can be
    checked independently (run it, count rows, eyeball the output — the grain
    habit made cheap); the final query reads as a story of steps rather than a
    cathedral of nested subqueries; and debugging means isolating one stage
    instead of unpicking parentheses. The style rules that matter in practice:
    one logical step per CTE; filter early (smaller intermediate sets, faster
    plans); name the grain in a comment; and prefer explicit column lists in
    anything shared (SELECT * breaks silently when upstream schemas change —
    the cleaning lesson's rot, arriving in your query). Analysis SQL is read by
    humans and machines; structure it accordingly.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked analysis: from business question to query</h2>
  <p>
    Watch the techniques assemble on a real question: "Which customer segments
    grew fastest last quarter, and what did they buy?" — the full analyst arc in
    one CTE chain. Step 1, define the population: orders in the quarter with
    status completed (a WHERE with a documented filter — returns excluded, and
    the exclusion stated). Step 2, establish grain: aggregate to
    customer-segment-month (SUM of amount) <em>before</em> joining segment
    attributes, so the fan-out never happens. Step 3, compute growth with a
    window: LAG over the segment's months, growth = (this − last) / last, guarded
    against division by zero (a NULL-safe case). Step 4, rank within segment:
    the window function again — fastest-growing segments first. Step 5, attach
    the "what did they buy" layer: join the top customers' order lines to a
    product dimension and aggregate to category, this time as a separate CTE
    with its own grain comment. Five stages, each checkable: the analyst runs
    each CTE standalone, counts rows, eyeballs ten rows — the grain habit made
    into a loop. Total: maybe forty lines of SQL that a teammate can read,
    audit and extend — which is the actual deliverable, because an analysis
    nobody can re-run is an anecdote.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four notes from the working edge. <strong>The engine is a planner</strong>:
    SQL describes results, and the optimiser chooses the execution plan —
    <code>EXPLAIN</code> (and ANALYZE variants) shows the plan, which is how you
    learn that your filter ran last, or that a join spilled to disk. Understanding
    that the plan, not the text, is the truth of a query separates senior
    analysts from intermediate ones. <strong>Indexes and selectivity</strong>:
    indexes turn scans into lookups on keys used for joins and filters — but only
    when selectivity is high (an index on status with three values helps nobody);
    filter before you join where you can, and index the join keys.
    <strong>Data modelling</strong>: warehouses are shaped deliberately — Kimball's
    star schema (facts, the events with measures; dimensions, the who/what/where
    context) is the analyst's map of a well-designed warehouse, and knowing it
    explains why your joins feel natural. <strong>SQL versus notebooks</strong>:
    the modern workflow handsoff to pandas or a BI tool for the last mile, but
    pushing aggregation into SQL (near the data, index-aware, cheap to schedule)
    and pulling small results out remains the performance and governance
    architecture of serious teams — the query is the contract, and the warehouse
    is where the truth lives.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Codd, "A Relational Model of Data for Large Shared Data Banks" (1970); PostgreSQL documentation; Mode SQL Tutorial; Kimball, The Data Warehouse Toolkit.</p>
